i'm bobby hundreds i'm the co-founder of  the hundreds i always say i do a little  bit of everything and a lot of nothing  and today i'm going to take you on a  tour of my collection  [Music]  as a designer and as a writer i'm most  inspired by drawing upon emotions and  nostalgia and sentimentality and so if  you look within the context of our brand  even  it's largely a nostalgia and  sentimentally based baseball cards that  would have probably been my first foray  into i'm actually collecting for the  specific purpose of collecting because  they might increase in value so baseball  cards then spoke to comic books and then  basketball cards came afterwards and so  i think i'm pretty much in everything  you know people ask me why i'm not into  watches i just don't wear watches i  don't like being mindful of time so  don't ever wait for  me this is just a fragment of everything  that i have i have even more  in rooms in the back and stashed away in  the archives and so sometimes i feel  like we have 90 000 square feet of  warehouse just to hold our collectibles  in our collections which is ridiculous  but within this immediate room i mean  you're looking at the breadth of my  entire life i'm looking around and i'm  seeing things that i had when i was five  and six years old there's garfield  household items  i'm a big garfield enthusiast of  anything i've collected in my life it's  probably garfield the most it started  with reading his comics and  me emulating jim davis the artist's  illustrations i always wanted to draw  cartoons and that was largely influenced  if not motivated by jim davis and  garfield secondarily to that jim davis  was also a master licensor the master of  licensing and  my childhood bedroom was garfield  pillowcases bed sheets erasers this was  my childhood clock the idea of my  favorite comic strip and cartoon not  being just something that i read and i  experience and interact with on a 2d  level but to be immersed in a garfield  world any way that i could consume and  collect garfield was really enjoyable  for me as a young kid because he was  like my best friend my attitude was  largely shaped by him i love lasagna to  this day because of garfield i don't  necessarily hate mondays but i've always  felt like when he says that he's just  being sarcastic i don't think he  actually hates anything and i think the  negativity is all a front with garfield  which  i drive with like i get that  i collect a lot of art and art comes in  all different forms and shapes and  ceramics have actually gotten quite  trendy over the last few years these are  sharif sharif is kind of leading the  charge and charging the lead he's just  the homie but also making these really  beautiful pieces of ceramics there's  just 10 pieces just chen is a really  incredible talented tattoo artist out of  canada but she's also gotten into  ceramics and a lot of this i think was  spurred if not inspired by what was  happening during the pandemic with  artists just trying to find different  lanes probably one of the most famous  and notable ceramics artists today is  seth rogen the actor seth has actually  gotten pretty proficient because he has  access  to the world's best ceramicist and so  he's learning from the best and he also  has access to the best materials and  tools but he really means it and he knew  that i had wanted one for so long and to  be a gift in one of these this is  probably one of my most prized  possessions if this entire place burns  down i'm going to come back for my seth  rogen piece right there  a lot of back to the future stuff when  we were younger there just weren't a lot  of back to the future memorabilia or  collectibles around and that generation  has grown up and the demand is there and  there's a marketplace for it it would  take me probably another lifetime to  configure this all you're looking at  some of it right here different bear  bricks and deloreans and uh  my wife got me that one for christmas  she actually got it for my son and then  i stole it because i was just like  you're not gonna care not the way that i  care about them i used to own the  world's best delorean it had 500 miles  on it it was preserved in a museum for  30 years before i bought it i don't have  it anymore it was using the bc boys last  music video it's been used in a bunch of  commercials i still miss the car and i  still miss the appeal of the design and  so i have a bunch of replica versions of  them from different parts of the film  series the gold delorean there are two  in the world it was part of an american  express campaign in 1981.  transformers did a 35th anniversary  version finally took them long enough to  make a time machine  the sneakers that i own people might  expect or anticipate that they're going  to be the most sensational sneakers out  there really expensive shoes that are  you know headline grabbers but they're  not  my favorite pair of sneakers in my  collection are always going to be my  converse chuck taylors and all stars the  end end-all be-all of what a good  sneaker is this is the alpha and omega  it's reduced down to its most classic  lines and shapes and you know nike is  embodied by a swoosh a converse all-star  like this is the logo the entire product  design it's really good for me  personally to look down at my feet and  just remember at the end of the day that  it's just canvas and rubber and we're  here to design and do good work but to  bring us back to our basics  i have air jordan 4s here because black  forest to me are the perfect sneaker  when i think of sneaker culture and  sneaker collecting you can't be in the  space without having black fours it  speaks to an air of design it's really  period specific but it also has  transcended multiple generations so in  the year 2022  an air jordan 4 i think is still really  relevant and meaningful and has its  place in sneaker culture  i have to speak on these when you scrub  through sneaker history you might be  hard-pressed to find many people  mentioning these but they speak to a  really specific  person who grew up in the 80s and 90s  and remember this period of reebok pump  when they were  larger than nike air it seems super  gimmicky now but to issue a sneaker for  tennis and the pump has been translated  into a fuzzy yellow tennis ball that was  just such an  innovative design choice  michael chang an asian american tennis  player who they were trying to position  as a rock star up against andre agassi  he was like the asian michael jordan to  me these are like asian jordans everyone  needs to know this was the asian  american jordan right here  you know this is just an homage to  virgil when he was dropping these shoes  i reached out to him to ask you know for  the connect and the plug and with  virgil's passing i  it's still really bizarre to me and to  many of us it feels like he's still very  present within  the culture and also within the universe  and i think it's because his design has  become so ubiquitous in our lives right  and so sneaker design and product design  and fashion have forever been impacted  if not the course has changed because of  virgil's fingerprint on it when i look  at these and i wonder what this is going  to do what it did for that generation of  sneakers over the last 10 years and then  what we will continue to do over the  forthcoming decades and how young people  will remember virgil's era of design  this is my way of understanding that  virgil and his work is immortal but of  all the sneakers that he worked on i  would say the prestos were my favorite  this is a very very famous chain it is  the hundreds ben baller chain it's just  funny because half the people believe  that it's real and half the people  thought it was fake and no matter what i  tell them they think it's the opposite  so to the people who believe it's real i  tell them  actually it's fake or it's real and  they're like  no it's probably the other way 